# builtbytyler.com — response headers for Cloudflare Pages.
# GitHub Pages cannot send these at all; this file is why we move.
# CSP host list was derived by scanning the repo, not guessed.
#
# Every header under a path must be INDENTED. Cloudflare's parser is
# lenient about it today, but an unindented line is a path pattern by the
# spec, so leaning on that leniency is how a security header silently
# stops being sent.
/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), autoplay=(), camera=(), display-capture=(), encrypted-media=(), fullscreen=(self), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), midi=(), payment=(self "https://js.stripe.com" "https://checkout.stripe.com"), usb=(), xr-spatial-tracking=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self' https://checkout.stripe.com https://buy.stripe.com; script-src 'self' https://js.stripe.com https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; script-src-attr 'none'; frame-src https://js.stripe.com https://checkout.stripe.com https://hooks.stripe.com; connect-src 'self' https://api.stripe.com https://checkout.stripe.com https://cloudflareinsights.com; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://*.stripe.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; media-src 'self'; worker-src 'self' blob:; manifest-src 'self'; upgrade-insecure-requests

# Code and styles are cache-busted by tools/version-assets.py, which stamps
# ?v=N on every reference at deploy time, so a returning visitor never pairs
# new HTML with an old stylesheet.
#
# A WEEK, NOT A YEAR, AND THE REASON MATTERS. A query string does not change
# the FILE PATH, so during a deploy the previous deployment will happily
# answer a request for products.js?v=23 with the old bytes — and whatever the
# edge caches at that moment is what it keeps. That happened on the v23 ship:
# a check fired seconds after the push, the edge stored the pre-deploy file
# under the new URL, and every visitor got the old catalogue. Under a
# one-year immutable rule that is a year-long bug caused by a race lasting
# seconds. Real content-hashed filenames do not have this problem, because
# the old deployment 404s the new name instead of serving stale bytes.
#
# Seven days keeps effectively all of the benefit — a returning visitor
# inside a week still skips the download, and anyone returning after months
# pays for one fetch — while bounding that failure to something that heals
# itself. Do not raise this without moving to hashed filenames first.
/assets/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, immutable

/assets/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, immutable

/assets/vendor/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, immutable

# Images are NOT stamped — the icons and the headshot are referenced by a
# bare path — so they get a long cache rather than a permanent one. A month
# is the most that can be given to a URL whose contents may be replaced.
/assets/img/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000
